WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:06.130
AccessAbility/VisAbility:
Breaking Down The Barriers

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:08.770
For People With
Disabilities In Media.

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:12.130
Please welcome Academy Governor,
and Oscar-winning actor,

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:13.120
Whoopi Goldberg.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:14.578
[AUDIO DESCRIPTION]
Whoopi Goldberg

00:00:14.578 --> 00:00:16.900
is a Black woman with shoulder
length black dreadlocks,

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.570
brown eyes, and is wearing
purple round glasses

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:21.340
and silver hoop earrings.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:23.650
She is wearing a very
light lavender colored top

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:25.810
with white buttons
and a black necklace,

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:27.400
with blue and white beads.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.140
And she is sitting
in a white chair

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:31.910
against a textured background.

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:34.160
Hey, I'm Whoopi
Goldberg and I am

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:37.400
proud to be a Governor of the
Academy of Motion Picture Arts

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.100
and Sciences, the Actors Branch.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:44.750
2020 marks the 30th
anniversary of the Americans

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:46.700
with Disabilities Act.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:50.120
It's a civil rights law,
prohibiting discrimination

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:55.230
against individuals with
disabilities in all life areas,

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:58.400
including jobs,
schools, transportation,

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.440
and public and
private places that

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.550
are open to the general public.

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:06.020
It guarantees equal
opportunity for all individuals

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:10.350
with disabilities, regardless
of race, color, sex,

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:13.400
national origin,
age and religion.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:16.820
One in five Americans
have a disability,

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:20.840
making them a part of America's
largest minority group.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:26.470
But not all disabilities
are immediately apparent.

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:29.500
In this program, we will
hear from an immensely

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:33.580
talented and diverse group of
industry professionals, actors

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:38.140
and managers, directors,
sound designers, VFX artists,

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.710
animators, all who happen
to have a disability.

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:44.200
So thank you to all
our panelists and a big

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:47.140
thank you to the Ruderman
Family Foundation

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:50.410
for the grant which makes
this program possible.

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:54.850
I think you'll agree, yes,
we can always do better.

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:58.600
But we hope these discussions
will help educate and highlight

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:01.840
the importance and
advantages of hiring people

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:05.165
with disabilities, whether
it be in front of the camera,

00:02:05.165 --> 00:02:06.040
or behind the scenes.

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:11.820
Please welcome our host,
Oscar-winning actor,

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:14.885
Marlee Matlin, and
interpreter, Jack Jason.

00:02:14.885 --> 00:02:16.260
[AUDIO DESCRIPTION]
Marlee Matlin

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:18.000
is a white woman with
below the shoulder

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.310
length blonde hair
and blue eyes.

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.530
She is wearing a solid white
top, gold hoop earrings,

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:25.080
and a gold necklace.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:27.840
She is sitting in a multicolored
chair in an office space

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:29.880
in front of a large
blue and white painting,

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.385
some photographs,
her Oscar statuette

00:02:32.385 --> 00:02:33.510
and white and yellow roses.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:39.100
While a rarity in
motion pictures,

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:40.930
authentic disability
representation

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:43.690
can be traced back
to the silent era.

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:47.680
Films including, You'd Be
Surprised in 1926, The Best

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:51.910
Years of Our Lives in 1946,
Children of a Lesser God,

00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:59.140
1986, When I Walk, in
2013, The Writer in 2017,

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:04.570
The Silent Child in 2017,
A Quiet Place in 2018,

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:07.540
Give Me Liberty in 2019,
and The Peanut Butter

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:14.140
Falcon in 2019, featured
lived disability experiences.

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:18.220
Let's hear from actor Danny
Woodburn, and actor comedian,

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:22.150
Maysoon Zayid, and talent
manager, Eryn Brown,

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:25.690
about authentic
representation, why it matters

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:30.030
and how far we have come.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:32.790
[AUDIO DESCRIPTION] Authentic
Representation: Why It Matters

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:36.390
And How Far We Have Come,
with actor Danny Woodburn,

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:39.650
actor and comedian, Maysoon
Zayid, and talent manager Eryn

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:40.150
Brown.

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:42.490
Hi, everyone.

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:47.090
I'm Danny Woodburn, actor,
comedian, and activist.

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:51.960
I'm a white male
man with dwarfism.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:54.210
I am wearing a blue
