00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:10.750
Next up we have talent
manager, Eryn Brown.

00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:13.182
Eryn, is partner
at Management 360,

00:05:13.182 --> 00:05:15.640
and has guided the careers of
critically acclaimed artists,

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:20.350
such as Academy Award-winner,
Sebastian Lelio of A Fantastic

00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:24.690
Woman, Pablo Larrain of Jackie,
Phil Johnston of Wreck-It Ralph

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:28.510
and Zootopia, and Cate
Shortland of Black Widow.

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:30.040
My name is Eryn Brown.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:35.000
I am a 46-year-old white
woman with light brown hair.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:40.300
I was born with an
undiagnosed muscular myopathy.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:43.300
I'm wearing a blue
and white pinstripe

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:47.770
shirt with a ruffled color
and a bubblegum pink cardigan.

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:50.560
Behind me, over
my right shoulder

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:54.505
is wallpaper with blue
and pink palm fronds,

00:05:54.505 --> 00:06:00.490
and over my left shoulder is
a glass wall with more palms.

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:03.090
I didn't get as descriptive
with my clothing as you are you

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:04.030
guys did.

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:06.522
But anyway, welcome to you both.

00:06:06.522 --> 00:06:10.270
We have a very interesting
panel, the panel,

00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:12.775
let's call it this: Authentic
Representation: Why It Matters

00:06:12.775 --> 00:06:13.870
And How Far We Have Come.

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:17.080
why it matters, and
how far we have come.

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:20.910
So the obvious first question
is, why does it matter

00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:22.300
and how far have we come?

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:25.930
We hear the hashtag all the
time, representation matters,

00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:29.530
but I want to talk about
why it actually matters.

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:32.590
People with disabilities
are 20% at least, maybe

00:06:32.590 --> 00:06:34.840
25% of the population.

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:39.070
But we're only 2% of the
images you see on TV.

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:44.650
And 95% of those images are
non disabled performers.

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:47.590
The message being sent
out to disabled kids

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:50.860
is, you do not
belong in this world.

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:53.950
It is a very, very
dangerous message.

00:06:53.950 --> 00:06:57.940
It adds to the stigma
against visible disabilities

00:06:57.940 --> 00:06:59.650
and invisible disabilities.

00:06:59.650 --> 00:07:02.110
Because we actually have
no idea how many people

00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:04.960
with invisible disabilities
grace our screens,

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:09.430
because the stigma is so strong,
that even celebrities feel

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:13.760
fear revealing that they're part
of the disability community.

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:15.940
So one of the reasons
I think it matters,

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:18.010
is because people
with disabilities

00:07:18.010 --> 00:07:22.000
face enormous amounts of
bullying, of violence,

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:23.570
and of discrimination.

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:27.580
And I believe that positive
images of disability,

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:33.100
in both fiction and nonfiction
can help stop the violence

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:34.930
that we as a community face.

00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:38.170
My personal feeling is
that, in the last five years

00:07:38.170 --> 00:07:42.300
I have felt an exponential shift
compared to the previous 20.

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:43.000
I don't know.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:44.510
Do you guys feel
the same way that--

00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:47.350
I feel like we're
in so much trouble

00:07:47.350 --> 00:07:49.490
and that's gotten so much worse.

00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:51.950
So you are as you said, the
voice of doom, but I think--

00:07:51.950 --> 00:07:54.055
I'm the goddess of
darkness, but what

00:07:54.055 --> 00:07:58.220
I do see of disability on
screen, is mostly white men.

00:07:58.220 --> 00:08:01.840
So that's not really feeling
very representative to me,

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:04.210
as a woman of color,
or someone you

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:06.100
know who works in
the intersectionality

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:07.280
of the community.

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:09.400
I think that the images
that we are seeing

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:14.350
are still too infantilized,
too much inspiration porn.

00:08:14.350 --> 00:08:17.080
And I know, I know that
the stories are not

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:18.760
being told by us.

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:21.700
I still feel like
anytime I enter

00:08:21.700 --> 00:08:25.840
a room, a studio, or a set,
I'm the only disabled person.

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:28.570
It's daunting being the
only disabled person,

00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:32.200
and I don't really feel
like we've made progress

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:38.380
until big stars finally say no
to playing disabled on screen,

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:40.480
if they are not
disabled in real life.

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:43.150
Once we have people
saying no to that,

00:08:43.150 --> 00:08:45.940
then I'll think
we've moved forward.

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:48.310
Eryn, do you feel
like as a person that

00:08:48.310 --> 00:08:53.545
works with creatives, as
a person who essentially

00:08:53.545 --> 00:08:57.350
is connecting to
decision makers, right?

00:08:57.350 --> 00:09:00.840
Writers, producers, do you
feel like there is a shift

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:04.270
or how do you feel about where
we are, where we've come from?

00:09:04.270 --> 00:09:07.210
I guess I have to
agree with Maysoon,

00:09:07.210 --> 00:09:11.620
I've never seen
myself on television.

00:09:11.620 --> 00:09:14.770
And the great
surprise for me was

00:09:14.770 --> 00:09:17.380
that I didn't realize
that I had never

00:09:17.380 --> 00:09:22.000
seen myself on TV, until
a couple of years ago

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:25.150
when a colleague took me to
the premiere of his client's,

00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:30.580
"Special", which is a wonderful
series written and created

00:09:30.580 --> 00:09:32.710
and starring Ryan O'Connell.

00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:38.260
And it was the first time
that I saw a disabled person

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:45.400
just existing in their life, and
doing normal things like dating

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:47.530
and having fights with
their friends and squabbles

00:09:47.530 --> 00:09:48.550
with their parents.

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:52.120
And I'm not young.

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:55.600
I'm not a man, and
I'm not gay, but that

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:58.360
was the first time
I felt like I'd even

00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:01.000
seen a shred of something
that I related to.

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:03.460
Do you feel like you have
any influence over the talent

00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:07.330
that you represent, like, just
your mere presence or them

00:10:07.330 --> 00:10:09.010
connecting to you on
a different level?

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:11.052
Has a writer ever come to
you and said, look, I'm

00:10:11.052 --> 00:10:13.840
writing this story, what
do you think of this?

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:14.890
They ask your opinion.

00:10:14.890 --> 00:10:17.340
There's not other disabled
writers in the room.

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:21.880
Will they ask your opinion
as maybe their only resource?

00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:23.380
No, they haven't.

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:26.380
I do feel that I have
a lot of influence,

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:31.510
and I take very seriously the
responsibility, my contribution

00:10:31.510 --> 00:10:35.120
to the culture
that I help create

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:37.120
through the work of my clients.

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:43.300
But the stigma of disability
is so strong and so shameful

00:10:43.300 --> 00:10:47.950
that there has been a lack
of discourse about it,

00:10:47.950 --> 00:10:51.290
even between me
and my own clients,

00:10:51.290 --> 00:10:58.750
because as I do gently try to
explore that and suggest when

00:10:58.750 --> 00:11:03.400
people are talking about
LGBTQ, or BIPOC characters,

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:06.040
I'll say, what about
someone in a wheelchair?

00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:07.720
But it's a fine line.

00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:09.490
I don't want anyone
to think that I'm

00:11:09.490 --> 00:11:13.760
servicing a particular
agenda or one of my own.

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:14.260
Right.

00:11:14.260 --> 00:11:18.490
Well, for me growing up, I
know you said you don't really

00:11:18.490 --> 00:11:19.720
see yourself represented.

00:11:19.720 --> 00:11:22.000
And the times that
I did see myself,

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:24.820
I grew up in the
'70s predominantly,

00:11:24.820 --> 00:11:27.490
it's where I think, the
formative years occurred for me

00:11:27.490 --> 00:11:30.230
and seeing somebody like Michael
Dunn, who was a little person

00:11:30.230 --> 00:11:31.990
actor, or Billy Barty on screen.

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:34.860
A lot of times it
came with pathos.

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:37.570
It filled in a lot of
the blanks for the tropes

00:11:37.570 --> 00:11:40.240
around disability.

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:44.442
The sad little man, or
the devious little man,

00:11:44.442 --> 00:11:45.400
or something like that.

00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:48.310
I don't know that there
were very many positives.

00:11:48.310 --> 00:11:50.230
I appreciated
their performances.

00:11:50.230 --> 00:11:53.840
I appreciated what they
were able to do as actors,

00:11:53.840 --> 00:11:56.590
but a lot of times the
storylines were the same.

00:11:56.590 --> 00:11:59.050
I connected more to somebody
like Sydney Poitier,

00:11:59.050 --> 00:12:02.080
who for me was like
a powerful influence

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:05.770
on, in my sense, what it
meant to be accepted as a man.

00:12:05.770 --> 00:12:08.860
I think that really is
like a big reality that

00:12:08.860 --> 00:12:11.230
still remains prevalent.

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:13.740
Is that like disabled
people only get three

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:15.370
storylines, right?

00:12:15.370 --> 00:12:20.320
You can't love me because I'm
disabled, heal me or kill me.

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:23.410
I grew up seeing a lot
of disabled stories

00:12:23.410 --> 00:12:27.610
where families were better off
when the disabled person died.

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:31.660
Or I saw a lot of disabled
people being healed.

00:12:31.660 --> 00:12:34.430
And I always talk about
this one image that

00:12:34.430 --> 00:12:36.520
stuck with me
forever, was there was

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:39.190
a blind character on "Little
House on the Prairie",

00:12:39.190 --> 00:12:41.620
and he got caught
in an explosion,

00:12:41.620 --> 00:12:43.390
and he got his vision back.

00:12:43.390 --> 00:12:47.640
And like, I'm really glad I
didn't blow myself up as a kid,

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:48.910
trying to heal myself.

00:12:48.910 --> 00:12:51.220
Like these things
are really dangerous

00:12:51.220 --> 00:12:54.160
and we're just like
inundated with these images

00:12:54.160 --> 00:12:57.430
that we either have
to be charity cases

00:12:57.430 --> 00:13:01.270
where you get a blanket if
you give us money or kill us

00:13:01.270 --> 00:13:02.950
and your life will be better.

00:13:02.950 --> 00:13:06.730
And I think we're still living
in those three stories, which

00:13:06.730 --> 00:13:10.750
is why I really do enjoy working
with both Eryn and Danny,

00:13:10.750 --> 00:13:13.870
because I think we really are
fighting for those mainstream

00:13:13.870 --> 00:13:14.470
images.

00:13:14.470 --> 00:13:18.010
Like Danny said, he was
influenced by Sidney Poitier.

00:13:18.010 --> 00:13:19.860
I was influenced
by Dolly Parton.

00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:22.720
And like the fact that she is
not disabled never occurred

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:25.270
to me, it was like, that
was the kind of entertainer

00:13:25.270 --> 00:13:26.590
I wanted to be.

00:13:26.590 --> 00:13:30.770
And I pursued it, and I
managed to navigate that world

00:13:30.770 --> 00:13:33.880
by going through comedy,
because I did not

00:13:33.880 --> 00:13:37.030
see people who looked
like me on television,

00:13:37.030 --> 00:13:39.370
except in the world of comedy.

00:13:39.370 --> 00:13:42.370
Because comedy is like
the home of misfits.

00:13:42.370 --> 00:13:43.030
Right.

00:13:43.030 --> 00:13:44.860
In my comedy career,
was basically

00:13:44.860 --> 00:13:47.860
I was told from early on,
you just do short jokes.

00:13:47.860 --> 00:13:52.180
It was never about addressing
the way society has

00:13:52.180 --> 00:13:54.430
been looking at me and how
it's been wrong, but how I

00:13:54.430 --> 00:13:56.080
should look at myself as wrong.

00:13:56.080 --> 00:13:58.840
So in my comedy, I flipped that.

00:13:58.840 --> 00:14:03.340
That's the challenge that
we have to dismantle.

00:14:03.340 --> 00:14:05.980
Is that the content
that's been made

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:08.540
suggests that we
are the problem.

00:14:08.540 --> 00:14:12.730
And when you internalize
that from a very young age,

00:14:12.730 --> 00:14:17.920
why would I bring it up to
people, whether it's my clients

00:14:17.920 --> 00:14:21.370
or otherwise, because I am
the problem in the world.

00:14:21.370 --> 00:14:23.320
The world isn't the
problem, it's me.

00:14:23.320 --> 00:14:25.030
We've tried everything.

00:14:25.030 --> 00:14:28.180
We've tried shaming actors
into not playing disabled

00:14:28.180 --> 00:14:29.350
if they're not disabled.

00:14:29.350 --> 00:14:31.660
We've tried showing
our buying power.

00:14:31.660 --> 00:14:33.850
We've shown that
we have the talent.

00:14:33.850 --> 00:14:37.750
We've shown that any project
we do ends up being successful.

00:14:37.750 --> 00:14:38.920
It's not working.

00:14:38.920 --> 00:14:41.330
I've been doing it for 20 years.

00:14:41.330 --> 00:14:44.530
And disabled people are
still being shunned.

00:14:44.530 --> 00:14:46.780
We're not allowed
to tell our stories,

00:14:46.780 --> 00:14:49.180
and we're simply not
included even close

00:14:49.180 --> 00:14:51.040
to the level that we need to be.

00:14:51.040 --> 00:14:55.570
So I wish I had some positive
story about never giving

00:14:55.570 --> 00:14:59.050
up and creating your own
stuff, but I don't know.

00:14:59.050 --> 00:15:02.920
I think it has to be something
where the unions demand

00:15:02.920 --> 00:15:06.160
equal representation,
and that if projects

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:08.320
aren't inclusive, and
aren't accessible,

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:12.370
then they don't get the status
that they need to go forward.

00:15:12.370 --> 00:15:16.210
It has to be like legislated
for it to actually happen.

00:15:16.210 --> 00:15:19.630
I don't think just trying to
convince Hollywood to give us

00:15:19.630 --> 00:15:22.240
a chance has worked
in the past 50 years,

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:25.000
and it doesn't look like
it's going to change

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:26.820
unless it's forced to.

00:15:26.820 --> 00:15:30.160
We still have such
an uphill battle

00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:32.500
because for the longest
time as you both know,

00:15:32.500 --> 00:15:35.050
I'm sure we have not been
included in the diversity

00:15:35.050 --> 00:15:36.040
discussion.

00:15:36.040 --> 00:15:39.900
And then when we do make a
little headway, for example,

00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:42.430
what the Academy
put out recently

00:15:42.430 --> 00:15:46.000
with regard to
nomination requirements,

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:49.040
there is tremendous pushback
from supposed progressives

00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:53.110
who see this as a
slight to the art.

00:15:53.110 --> 00:15:55.810
And they make these comments
without a full understanding

00:15:55.810 --> 00:15:59.740
of the scope of what has
gone on for years, and years,

00:15:59.740 --> 00:16:03.220
and years, and what
continues to go on unchanged.

00:16:03.220 --> 00:16:06.675
And art is supposed to be
all inclusive, is it not?

00:16:06.675 --> 00:16:08.800
And they don't have that
understanding of that part

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:10.070
of the conversation.

00:16:10.070 --> 00:16:11.660
I think there's so
many layers to it

00:16:11.660 --> 00:16:16.060
and I think a raised
awareness in this moment

00:16:16.060 --> 00:16:19.660
of cultural reckoning
is really imperative,

00:16:19.660 --> 00:16:22.510
and a pointing out,
include disability

00:16:22.510 --> 00:16:27.700
in the conversation when there
are conversations about LGBTQ

00:16:27.700 --> 00:16:32.795
and BIPOC that disability is a
category, that is automatically

00:16:32.795 --> 00:16:33.295
discussed.

00:16:33.295 --> 00:16:34.978
So it becomes normalized.

00:16:34.978 --> 00:16:36.520
And then I think
that there will have

00:16:36.520 --> 00:16:39.970
to be something that's
a big disruptor.

00:16:39.970 --> 00:16:44.710
I agree with Maysoon, and I
don't know if it's legislation

00:16:44.710 --> 00:16:50.380
or certain companies decide
that they want to support,

00:16:50.380 --> 00:16:54.670
and they want to get behind
a movement that says,

00:16:54.670 --> 00:16:59.157
not only this is the right thing
to do, but it's good business.

00:16:59.157 --> 00:16:59.740
Good business.

00:16:59.740 --> 00:17:05.020
It has really positive
cultural ramifications

00:17:05.020 --> 00:17:10.569
and it's opportunity for
an intersectional movement

00:17:10.569 --> 00:17:16.180
because disability affects every
group and anyone at any moment

00:17:16.180 --> 00:17:18.319
can become disabled.

00:17:18.319 --> 00:17:22.839
So it's in everyone's best
interest for the world

00:17:22.839 --> 00:17:25.540
to be accommodating.

00:17:25.540 --> 00:17:28.089
Adam Sandler told me
the best thing ever,

00:17:28.089 --> 00:17:31.090
because I know it's really
hard for disabled people

00:17:31.090 --> 00:17:32.620
to ask for accommodations.

00:17:32.620 --> 00:17:34.630
You finally make it
through the door,

00:17:34.630 --> 00:17:37.780
and the last thing you
want to do is be a burden,

00:17:37.780 --> 00:17:39.580
delay any shooting.

00:17:39.580 --> 00:17:42.820
And I was having trouble getting
in and out of my trailer.

00:17:42.820 --> 00:17:46.420
So I was basically
jumping into a PA's arms.

00:17:46.420 --> 00:17:49.960
And Adam Sandler saw me, and he
was like, "What is happening?

00:17:49.960 --> 00:17:52.150
Make her trailer accessible."

00:17:52.150 --> 00:17:54.820
And I said to him,
I really didn't

00:17:54.820 --> 00:17:56.170
want to be high maintenance.

00:17:56.170 --> 00:18:00.100
And he said, look around,
we were in Hollywood,

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:02.920
I was on the set
with Mariah Carey,

00:18:02.920 --> 00:18:05.770
I was not the person who
was high maintenance.

00:18:05.770 --> 00:18:08.470
So we have to get
rid of that fear

00:18:08.470 --> 00:18:12.010
of asking but also
understand that we

00:18:12.010 --> 00:18:14.080
will be met with resistance.

00:18:14.080 --> 00:18:16.780
So even though it's
not our job, it's

00:18:16.780 --> 00:18:19.330
good when you're asking
for accommodations

00:18:19.330 --> 00:18:22.600
to know what the solution is.

00:18:22.600 --> 00:18:26.200
Even better, it shouldn't
be your responsibility

00:18:26.200 --> 00:18:28.810
to ask for accommodations.

00:18:28.810 --> 00:18:32.110
It should be part
of the contract,

00:18:32.110 --> 00:18:36.280
and part of the line producer,
and a part of the production,

00:18:36.280 --> 00:18:40.590
where they ask you, what
do you need to do your job?

00:18:40.590 --> 00:18:41.380
Right.

00:18:41.380 --> 00:18:43.030
It's just not--

00:18:43.030 --> 00:18:44.890
I think it's also
has to do with how --

00:18:44.890 --> 00:18:46.390
forget the stigma
around disability.

00:18:46.390 --> 00:18:47.682
There's a stigma around actors.

00:18:47.682 --> 00:18:48.500
Right?

00:18:48.500 --> 00:18:51.010
I think we need a
disabled Disney princess.

00:18:51.010 --> 00:18:54.850
So we do have one Disney
disability icon, Nemo.

00:18:54.850 --> 00:18:56.140
He's got the bum fin.

00:18:56.140 --> 00:19:01.450
And there's-- a belief that
Lilo &amp; Stitch is also a disabled

00:19:01.450 --> 00:19:02.170
story.

00:19:02.170 --> 00:19:06.310
I think it'd be really helpful
to have a disabled princess.

00:19:06.310 --> 00:19:08.200
I want to end on
a positive note,

00:19:08.200 --> 00:19:10.570
because I was like
so doom and gloom.

00:19:10.570 --> 00:19:13.500
A lot of disabled
people are putting out

00:19:13.500 --> 00:19:16.230
a lot of fantastic work.

00:19:16.230 --> 00:19:17.790
It's amazing.

00:19:17.790 --> 00:19:20.130
Danny and I are
producing this show.

00:19:20.130 --> 00:19:23.850
And the number of new
talent applications we got

00:19:23.850 --> 00:19:24.980
was astounding.

00:19:24.980 --> 00:19:27.690
And the amount of
just pure talent

00:19:27.690 --> 00:19:30.630
in this community
that exists is ready

00:19:30.630 --> 00:19:33.630
for their close-uo
is really inspiring.

00:19:33.630 --> 00:19:38.550
I'm seeing disabled people
in every area excelling.

00:19:38.550 --> 00:19:40.860
We just need to make
sure they get the chance.

00:19:40.860 --> 00:19:45.220
It's not a lack of talented
disabled people in the world,

00:19:45.220 --> 00:19:49.020
it's just the lack of the
ability to showcase it.

00:19:49.020 --> 00:19:51.060
And I think too.

00:19:51.060 --> 00:19:53.740
The people that we get
that have the talent,

00:19:53.740 --> 00:19:56.310
I think the powers that
be have to recognize,

00:19:56.310 --> 00:20:00.000
they fought harder for their
education than anybody else.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:03.120
And they did all that they
could to learn their craft

00:20:03.120 --> 00:20:04.530
and be exceptional.

00:20:04.530 --> 00:20:09.960
And so I feel like that--- we
had to go that extra mile that

00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:12.180
a non disabled person
doesn't have to go,

00:20:12.180 --> 00:20:16.320
to get to that place of being
accepted as a viable talent.

00:20:16.320 --> 00:20:17.790
That, I think
should be something

00:20:17.790 --> 00:20:21.060
on the minds of people in
the position of casting

00:20:21.060 --> 00:20:23.400
or producing a project.

00:20:23.400 --> 00:20:25.740
That they go, oh,
this person really

00:20:25.740 --> 00:20:28.320
has come a long way to
get to this spot, where

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:29.910
I'm interested in
what they're doing.

00:20:29.910 --> 00:20:31.650
And it's an unfortunate
reality that we

00:20:31.650 --> 00:20:34.650
have to go that extra mile but
I think if they have a better

00:20:34.650 --> 00:20:36.810
understanding that we've
gone the extra mile,

00:20:36.810 --> 00:20:42.220
then we'll be in a place where
these panels are obsolete.

00:20:42.220 --> 00:20:44.100
I don't want our
panels to be obsolete.

00:20:44.100 --> 00:20:48.060
This is the most fun I've had
since the pandemic started.

00:20:48.060 --> 00:20:51.540
I think that one thing that
would really change everything,

00:20:51.540 --> 00:20:55.110
is if me and Danny
co-hosted the next Oscars.

00:20:55.110 --> 00:20:58.020
That would be the
disability representation

00:20:58.020 --> 00:21:02.280
that pushed everyone to have
equal disabled representation

00:21:02.280 --> 00:21:03.215
on all their shows.

00:21:03.215 --> 00:21:04.173
I think that's the key.

00:21:04.173 --> 00:21:07.980
I couldn't have said a better
idea if I had a year and a half

00:21:07.980 --> 00:21:09.390
to come up with it.

00:21:09.390 --> 00:21:10.920
Brilliant idea.

00:21:10.920 --> 00:21:13.070
I wonder who we could talk to?!

00:21:13.070 --> 00:21:14.748
I wonder?!

00:21:14.748 --> 00:21:16.290
I think it's
interesting to point out

00:21:16.290 --> 00:21:21.230
that I think there have been
62 nominees for characters

00:21:21.230 --> 00:21:24.190
with disability over
the Oscar's history.

00:21:24.190 --> 00:21:29.510
There have been 27 winners of
that category for best actors

00:21:29.510 --> 00:21:31.350
or supporting actor, actress.

00:21:31.350 --> 00:21:34.120
But only two had
actual disability.

00:21:34.120 --> 00:21:37.560
So what better way to
shift the paradigm,

00:21:37.560 --> 00:21:39.886
than to have Maysoon
and I host the show.

00:21:39.886 --> 00:21:41.760
I'll do your hair and makeup.

00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:42.790
Oh, cool.

00:21:42.790 --> 00:21:43.290
Awesome.

00:21:43.290 --> 00:21:45.480
You know how to do
lost Kardashian?

00:21:45.480 --> 00:21:46.588
I'll learn.

00:21:46.588 --> 00:21:48.630
And cruise ship captain,
you know how to do that?

00:21:48.630 --> 00:21:49.710
And cruise ship captain.

00:21:49.710 --> 00:21:50.210
Yeah.

00:21:50.210 --> 00:21:51.630
All right.

00:21:51.630 --> 00:21:52.740
Thank you, both so much.

00:21:52.740 --> 00:21:53.740
I'm glad we could do it.

00:21:53.740 --> 00:21:55.320
Thank you so much for having us.

00:21:55.320 --> 00:21:56.977
And thanks to the Academy.

00:22:00.880 --> 00:22:05.360
And now please welcome
back Marlee Matlin.

00:22:05.360 --> 00:22:08.180
Not only do inclusion
riders call for diversity

00:22:08.180 --> 00:22:11.450
in front of the
camera, but they also

00:22:11.450 --> 00:22:16.670
strive for equity and
inclusivity behind the scenes.

00:22:16.670 --> 00:22:20.120
Employers might be hesitant
to hire industry professionals

00:22:20.120 --> 00:22:23.450
with disabilities because
of additional costs.

00:22:23.450 --> 00:22:26.570
But incorporating
accessibility measures,

00:22:26.570 --> 00:22:30.560
open up employers to a pool
of qualified applicants,

00:22:30.560 --> 00:22:33.860
often overlooked in the past.

00:22:33.860 --> 00:22:38.700
Filmmakers, Jenni Gold, Jim
LeBrecht, and visual effects

00:22:38.700 --> 00:22:41.880
supervisor, Kaitlyn
Yang, have created

00:22:41.880 --> 00:22:44.820
successful and
accessible businesses

00:22:44.820 --> 00:22:49.050
for below the line
professionals with disabilities.

00:22:49.050 --> 00:22:51.390
Let's hear from them
about their work,

00:22:51.390 --> 00:22:56.990
behind the camera, why
inclusion benefits us all.

00:22:56.990 --> 00:23:00.780
Behind The Camera: Why
Inclusion Benefits Us All.

00:23:00.780 --> 00:23:03.690
With filmmakers, Jenni
Gold and Jim LeBrecht,

00:23:03.690 --> 00:23:07.010
and VFX supervisor,
Kaitlyn Yang.

00:23:07.010 --> 00:23:08.750
Hi, I'm Jenni Gold.

00:23:08.750 --> 00:23:11.697
I have blonde hair
and brown eyes.

00:23:11.697 --> 00:23:13.280
I'm wearing a red
shirt with a pattern

00:23:13.280 --> 00:23:16.910
and I'm sitting in a power
wheelchair in my home theater.

00:23:16.910 --> 00:23:21.440
You might know me best from the
film I produced and directed,

00:23:21.440 --> 00:23:24.080
the award winning
star-studded CinemAbility:

00:23:24.080 --> 00:23:25.870
the Art of Inclusion.

00:23:25.870 --> 00:23:28.190
And now let me introduce
two good friends

00:23:28.190 --> 00:23:30.020
and remarkable people.

00:23:30.020 --> 00:23:32.900
First, the talented
James LeBrecht

00:23:32.900 --> 00:23:35.680
who has more than 35
years of experience

00:23:35.680 --> 00:23:39.260
as a film and theater
sound designer and mixer.

00:23:39.260 --> 00:23:42.700
He is also a founder of
Berkeley Sound Artists,

00:23:42.700 --> 00:23:46.250
an audio post-production
company and the co-director

00:23:46.250 --> 00:23:50.120
and co-producer with Nicole
Newnham of the Netflix

00:23:50.120 --> 00:23:52.700
documentary Crip Camp.

00:23:52.700 --> 00:23:54.836
Hey, Jim, welcome.

00:23:54.836 --> 00:23:56.090
Hi, Jenni.

00:23:56.090 --> 00:23:57.550
Great to be here.

00:23:57.550 --> 00:24:02.760
I am a older white
guy with a gray goatee

00:24:02.760 --> 00:24:04.930
and kind of brownish long hair.

00:24:04.930 --> 00:24:07.410
And I've got a blue
patterned shirt on.

00:24:07.410 --> 00:24:10.230
I am in my home
office in Oakland.

00:24:10.230 --> 00:24:12.900
There's a red wall behind
me with some posters on it.

00:24:12.900 --> 00:24:15.630
And that about sums me up.

00:24:15.630 --> 00:24:18.600
Now I have the pleasure of
introducing visual effects

00:24:18.600 --> 00:24:23.610
supervisor Kaitlyn Yang, who is
the founder of Alpha Studios.

00:24:23.610 --> 00:24:26.310
Kaitlyn has more than
50 credits to her name,

00:24:26.310 --> 00:24:30.510
including Robot Chicken, Grey's
Anatomy, and Four Weddings

00:24:30.510 --> 00:24:31.800
and a Funeral.

00:24:31.800 --> 00:24:33.520
Thanks for joining us, Kaitlyn.

00:24:33.520 --> 00:24:34.200
Hey, Jenni.

00:24:34.200 --> 00:24:34.900
Hey, Jim.

00:24:34.900 --> 00:24:36.790
Thank you for having me.

00:24:36.790 --> 00:24:39.660
I am a Asian-American woman.

00:24:39.660 --> 00:24:42.870
I am at my office with
white walls behind me

00:24:42.870 --> 00:24:45.540
with some posters of
our most recent work.

00:24:45.540 --> 00:24:49.530
And as always, I'm surrounded
by computers and monitors.

00:24:49.530 --> 00:24:50.880
Thanks for joining us, Kaitlyn.

00:24:50.880 --> 00:24:53.790
And welcome, everyone, as we
take a look behind the camera

00:24:53.790 --> 00:24:57.420
to discuss why inclusion
benefits us all.

00:24:57.420 --> 00:24:59.220
So let's discuss it.

00:24:59.220 --> 00:25:02.760
Well, I think that the more
varied voices and experiences

00:25:02.760 --> 00:25:07.650
that come into a team working
on a film, the greater

00:25:07.650 --> 00:25:13.860
the possibility for bringing out
nuance in stories that really

00:25:13.860 --> 00:25:17.950
paint a brighter picture
to what we're doing.

00:25:17.950 --> 00:25:20.220
I think for me, you know,
right now in Hollywood,

00:25:20.220 --> 00:25:23.340
we are not only craving
for ourselves to be seen.

00:25:23.340 --> 00:25:26.430
But we're really craving stories
that we haven't heard before.

00:25:26.430 --> 00:25:30.660
And I think one great
treasure trove of stories

00:25:30.660 --> 00:25:33.150
that we haven't heard
before are from people

00:25:33.150 --> 00:25:35.700
who are underrepresented,
especially

00:25:35.700 --> 00:25:37.390
people with disabilities.

00:25:37.390 --> 00:25:41.080
And I think it makes
for great storytelling.

00:25:41.080 --> 00:25:44.270
It also makes great
business sense as well.

00:25:44.270 --> 00:25:45.270
Right?

00:25:45.270 --> 00:25:48.102
We kind of make
up 1/4 of America,

00:25:48.102 --> 00:25:49.560
which is kind of
crazy if you think

00:25:49.560 --> 00:25:51.630
about it, everyone
with a physical

00:25:51.630 --> 00:25:53.290
or invisible disability.

00:25:53.290 --> 00:25:56.520
And I think it's just
good business sense for us

00:25:56.520 --> 00:25:58.870
to be included not only
in front of the camera,

00:25:58.870 --> 00:26:00.930
but also behind the
camera so we can support

00:26:00.930 --> 00:26:03.630
one another with the dollars
that we're essentially

00:26:03.630 --> 00:26:07.470
voting whenever we're
going to support a movie.

00:26:07.470 --> 00:26:10.530
How can we help other
professionals make sure

00:26:10.530 --> 00:26:12.570
that when they're
hiring that they

00:26:12.570 --> 00:26:16.470
look for qualified candidates
who might have disabilities?

00:26:16.470 --> 00:26:19.590
I think in terms of
hiring, as we all know,

00:26:19.590 --> 00:26:22.770
the world is really not
made for people like us.

00:26:22.770 --> 00:26:24.600
If anything, we're
used to, as we're

00:26:24.600 --> 00:26:28.740
using to adapting our
environment for it to work

00:26:28.740 --> 00:26:31.560
for us, and that
transitions into so

00:26:31.560 --> 00:26:34.920
many transferable skills
especially in the filmmaking

00:26:34.920 --> 00:26:35.680
community.

00:26:35.680 --> 00:26:38.280
A lot of times you have
to think on your toes

00:26:38.280 --> 00:26:42.140
and come up with ideas and all
the good stuff that we are--

00:26:42.140 --> 00:26:45.340
we've been essentially
doing our whole lives.

00:26:45.340 --> 00:26:48.610
And I think for people
with disabilities,

00:26:48.610 --> 00:26:51.840
we do have to work
way harder than others

00:26:51.840 --> 00:26:54.210
to get a seat at the table.

00:26:54.210 --> 00:26:57.930
I would say that the
entry into our business,

00:26:57.930 --> 00:27:00.090
not only just
having a disability,

00:27:00.090 --> 00:27:03.490
but it comes down to
unpaid internships.

00:27:03.490 --> 00:27:06.790
There are plenty of people who
economically cannot do that.

00:27:06.790 --> 00:27:08.940
And they are being filtered out.

00:27:08.940 --> 00:27:11.160
And then when we look
at what qualifications

00:27:11.160 --> 00:27:14.700
we're looking for people
or how people kind of get

00:27:14.700 --> 00:27:18.210
into our business that we
have to kind of steer away

00:27:18.210 --> 00:27:22.170
from this idea of the entry
level position of somebody

00:27:22.170 --> 00:27:24.690
who is a production
assistant that

00:27:24.690 --> 00:27:29.580
can carry 14 cups of
coffee onto the set

00:27:29.580 --> 00:27:31.923
or that can work 20-hour days.

00:27:31.923 --> 00:27:33.840
And in the past, I think
it's been traditional

00:27:33.840 --> 00:27:35.798
that we think that if
you're not willing to put

00:27:35.798 --> 00:27:37.290
in the long, long
hours and stuff,

00:27:37.290 --> 00:27:41.520
then you really aren't
dedicated to working.

00:27:41.520 --> 00:27:44.550
And that's simply not true.

00:27:44.550 --> 00:27:47.640
Jim, I think you're hitting
so many great points there.

00:27:47.640 --> 00:27:50.010
I think one of them is
we've been overlooked

00:27:50.010 --> 00:27:52.170
for so long for a
lot of qualifications

00:27:52.170 --> 00:27:53.910
that we've been doing.

00:27:53.910 --> 00:27:56.980
As we navigate not only our
professional lives, but also

00:27:56.980 --> 00:28:00.120
in our personal lives as
well, I like to joke and say

00:28:00.120 --> 00:28:02.880
that we kind of show
up, and we kind of

00:28:02.880 --> 00:28:05.700
do BYOC, bring our
own chairs, right?

00:28:05.700 --> 00:28:07.923
And I think especially
for post-production,

00:28:07.923 --> 00:28:09.340
you're looking for
talented people

00:28:09.340 --> 00:28:11.860
to sit in front of a computer
all day problem solving.

00:28:11.860 --> 00:28:13.875
And that is our strength.

00:28:13.875 --> 00:28:15.000
You know, it's interesting.

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:18.030
I've come to realize
that my disability is

00:28:18.030 --> 00:28:22.200
an advantage for me in my
career choice as a director.

00:28:22.200 --> 00:28:26.050
You have to direct someone to
do what you want them to do,

00:28:26.050 --> 00:28:28.740
which is part of the skill
that I use on set with actors.

00:28:28.740 --> 00:28:32.070
I'm getting them to do what
I want them to do my way.

00:28:32.070 --> 00:28:33.430
I grew up doing that.

00:28:33.430 --> 00:28:37.300
And I think that a lot of people
do look at it as a negative,

00:28:37.300 --> 00:28:38.920
but it can be a positive.

00:28:38.920 --> 00:28:43.500
A phrase I hear a lot is I'm
a very precise communicator.

00:28:43.500 --> 00:28:46.380
And I say all the
information up front

00:28:46.380 --> 00:28:49.650
without you having to come back
and ask for clarifications.

00:28:49.650 --> 00:28:52.530
And I do this because
once again, I have

00:28:52.530 --> 00:28:54.540
been doing this my entire life.

00:28:54.540 --> 00:28:57.630
The ways that we're
describing what we need to see

00:28:57.630 --> 00:29:01.440
and the order that would be
most helpful for it to happen,

00:29:01.440 --> 00:29:03.540
that, again, is one
of our strengths.

00:29:03.540 --> 00:29:04.770
It's one of our expertise.

00:29:04.770 --> 00:29:09.270
And I think a lot of times
precise communication equals

00:29:09.270 --> 00:29:10.710
less communication.

00:29:10.710 --> 00:29:12.900
And it saves more
money if you can

00:29:12.900 --> 00:29:17.340
get someone who has a vision and
is very comfortable describing

00:29:17.340 --> 00:29:19.650
that particular vision
to an entire team

00:29:19.650 --> 00:29:21.730
like I do as a visual
effects supervisor.

00:29:21.730 --> 00:29:22.230
Right?

00:29:22.230 --> 00:29:24.930
That's my job on a daily basis.

00:29:24.930 --> 00:29:28.500
That will benefit the
post-production team overall.

00:29:28.500 --> 00:29:30.740
So Zoom is a big deal now.

00:29:30.740 --> 00:29:32.970
Everyone is using
it for meetings,

00:29:32.970 --> 00:29:37.320
and they're becoming more
comfortable with the idea.

00:29:37.320 --> 00:29:40.620
How do you think that is
affecting your meetings

00:29:40.620 --> 00:29:43.470
and your future
plans of continuing

00:29:43.470 --> 00:29:45.182
to work with it or not?

00:29:45.182 --> 00:29:46.890
So it's interesting
that you bring it up.

00:29:46.890 --> 00:29:50.550
One of the silver linings
I have found during COVID

00:29:50.550 --> 00:29:53.700
and taking a lot of meetings
via video conferencing

00:29:53.700 --> 00:29:56.580
on Zoom or other
platforms is I'm

00:29:56.580 --> 00:30:01.230
finding that video conferencing
is kind of taking away

00:30:01.230 --> 00:30:04.830
the uncomfortableness that
a lot of people might have

00:30:04.830 --> 00:30:06.660
if I were to take
the same meeting

00:30:06.660 --> 00:30:08.160
and I would go
into the boardroom,

00:30:08.160 --> 00:30:09.930
and I would roll
in, in my chair.

00:30:09.930 --> 00:30:14.280
Now I feel like Zoom is kind of
putting us on an equal playing

00:30:14.280 --> 00:30:17.445
field or getting closer to at
least where I can conference

00:30:17.445 --> 00:30:21.120
in with someone and not
having to worry about, oh,

00:30:21.120 --> 00:30:23.670
how do I address the
elephant in the room, right?

00:30:23.670 --> 00:30:26.340
The odds might be already
against you before you even

00:30:26.340 --> 00:30:27.270
show your ideas.

00:30:27.270 --> 00:30:30.210
But I think now on Zoom, we
can just start with the ideas.

00:30:30.210 --> 00:30:31.890
We can just start
with the deck, right?

00:30:31.890 --> 00:30:35.160
I don't necessarily have to feel
and take a temperature poll of,

00:30:35.160 --> 00:30:37.750
like, oh, how do people in
the Zoom call feel about--

00:30:37.750 --> 00:30:38.340
Right.

00:30:38.340 --> 00:30:41.010
--my wheelchair because
when I show up like this,

00:30:41.010 --> 00:30:45.240
those questions kind of get
erased temporarily, you know?

00:30:45.240 --> 00:30:47.310
I mean, I'll see kind of what--

00:30:47.310 --> 00:30:51.180
if their attitude translates
to in person when we do meet.

00:30:51.180 --> 00:30:53.730
But for now, Zoom
calls I think has

00:30:53.730 --> 00:30:57.050
been a great tool of
this equalizer in a way.

00:30:57.050 --> 00:30:59.970
Certainly starting out in my
audio post-production career,

00:30:59.970 --> 00:31:02.580
it was always like that
elephant in the room.

00:31:02.580 --> 00:31:04.410
I'm sitting in a wheelchair.

00:31:04.410 --> 00:31:09.270
And it was only by having
years and years of experience

00:31:09.270 --> 00:31:13.440
and building a reputation that
preceded me that I felt like I

00:31:13.440 --> 00:31:15.240
could really relax about that.

00:31:15.240 --> 00:31:17.760
When I first became a
member of the Academy,

00:31:17.760 --> 00:31:19.080
I came down to Los Angeles.

00:31:19.080 --> 00:31:22.320
And I wound up talking to
the head of Membership.

00:31:22.320 --> 00:31:26.260
And I was asking about
disability and percentages

00:31:26.260 --> 00:31:26.760
and stuff.

00:31:26.760 --> 00:31:29.850
And she told me, you
know, Jim, people are not

00:31:29.850 --> 00:31:33.270
willing to identify because
of the stigma of disability.

00:31:33.270 --> 00:31:36.040
The fact of the matter
is in an ideal world,

00:31:36.040 --> 00:31:40.080
if everybody revealed
or identified,

00:31:40.080 --> 00:31:42.360
people in our
industry would realize

00:31:42.360 --> 00:31:44.240
that we're everywhere really.

00:31:44.240 --> 00:31:46.360
You can see me and
Jenni and Kaitlyn.

00:31:46.360 --> 00:31:50.850
And yeah, you can tell that we
have some body jazz going on.

00:31:50.850 --> 00:31:53.370
But there's so many
different people

00:31:53.370 --> 00:32:00.360
who are right with you that
have to hide their disabilities.

00:32:00.360 --> 00:32:02.160
I totally agree, Jim.

00:32:02.160 --> 00:32:05.070
I think when Jenni and I first
connected many years ago,

00:32:05.070 --> 00:32:08.760
we first became bonded because
we have the same disability.

00:32:08.760 --> 00:32:11.790
We have spinal muscular atrophy.

00:32:11.790 --> 00:32:13.610
And I thought,
wow, how cool is it

00:32:13.610 --> 00:32:15.360
that I am seeing
someone else that

00:32:15.360 --> 00:32:19.270
is representing me and
fighting the same battle?

00:32:19.270 --> 00:32:22.040
I will just challenge
people and think about it.

00:32:22.040 --> 00:32:24.040
It's kind of like people
wearing glasses, right?

00:32:24.040 --> 00:32:27.270
Having that one tiny bit just
makes a world of difference.

00:32:27.270 --> 00:32:30.540
Our chairs make that world
of difference for us.

00:32:30.540 --> 00:32:33.840
For some other people, it might
be closed captioning, right?

00:32:33.840 --> 00:32:36.180
Maybe they are hard of hearing
at certain times, right?

00:32:36.180 --> 00:32:37.260
And that's helpful.

00:32:37.260 --> 00:32:38.880
And having image
description, that's

00:32:38.880 --> 00:32:40.860
also helpful for other people.

00:32:40.860 --> 00:32:43.560
It's so common, and
yet it's something

00:32:43.560 --> 00:32:47.130
that we're still not
sharing widely because it

00:32:47.130 --> 00:32:49.320
carries a stigma, right?

00:32:49.320 --> 00:32:51.360
I challenge you,
do you know anyone

00:32:51.360 --> 00:32:54.470
who doesn't have anything?

00:32:54.470 --> 00:32:55.000
Yeah.

00:32:55.000 --> 00:32:55.500
Yeah.

00:32:55.500 --> 00:32:59.610
It's nice to see that
people are starting

00:32:59.610 --> 00:33:04.980
to be more open and interested
in the whole inclusion

00:33:04.980 --> 00:33:06.450
discussions.

00:33:06.450 --> 00:33:09.420
Do you think that the
industry could do more than

00:33:09.420 --> 00:33:11.190
what it's doing currently?

00:33:11.190 --> 00:33:12.180
Absolutely.

00:33:12.180 --> 00:33:15.570
So I was bitten by
the visual effects

00:33:15.570 --> 00:33:18.090
bug at a very young age.

00:33:18.090 --> 00:33:21.060
Like most VFX people, I
was bitten by Star Wars.

00:33:21.060 --> 00:33:24.150
And I was absolutely blown
away at all the visuals

00:33:24.150 --> 00:33:24.750
I was seeing.

00:33:24.750 --> 00:33:26.910
And I even remember
from many decades

00:33:26.910 --> 00:33:29.490
ago when I was a
9-year-old, I was looking up

00:33:29.490 --> 00:33:31.620
visual effects in these books.

00:33:31.620 --> 00:33:34.890
And I couldn't find any
practitioners who were women.

00:33:34.890 --> 00:33:37.920
So one of the questions I
carried with me for a long time

00:33:37.920 --> 00:33:40.182
was, well, I hope
they're going to be OK

00:33:40.182 --> 00:33:42.390
having women work in this
field, because this is what

00:33:42.390 --> 00:33:44.220
I want to spend my life doing.

00:33:44.220 --> 00:33:47.880
And seeing the
data now in 2020, I

00:33:47.880 --> 00:33:51.330
think that is still a
legitimate question, right?

00:33:51.330 --> 00:33:54.060
There are still no data, no
official data coming out.

00:33:54.060 --> 00:33:58.610
But I did a quick data
gathering on LinkedIn.

00:33:58.610 --> 00:34:00.900
And the stats are not great.

00:34:00.900 --> 00:34:08.940
The stats I found is basically
0.001% of visual effects

00:34:08.940 --> 00:34:11.110
supervisors that are women.

00:34:11.110 --> 00:34:13.290
So we are still a
couple of digits

00:34:13.290 --> 00:34:15.480
of becoming a whole number.

00:34:15.480 --> 00:34:19.139
And if anyone else wants to
challenge me on those stats,

00:34:19.139 --> 00:34:20.730
I welcome them to, right?

00:34:20.730 --> 00:34:23.400
Because I would like
to know other women

00:34:23.400 --> 00:34:25.320
supervisors as well.

00:34:25.320 --> 00:34:27.510
I think you even beat
the female directors.

00:34:27.510 --> 00:34:29.420
I think that's even
worse than the numbers--

00:34:29.420 --> 00:34:30.215
Yeah, it's worse.

00:34:30.215 --> 00:34:31.590
I didn't think
that was possible,

00:34:31.590 --> 00:34:32.850
but I think you did it.

00:34:32.850 --> 00:34:35.190
I started something called
the Gold Test, which

00:34:35.190 --> 00:34:37.620
is similar to the Bechdel Test.

00:34:37.620 --> 00:34:41.820
That is the test about it if
there's two females in a movie

00:34:41.820 --> 00:34:45.150
and they talk to each
other about something other

00:34:45.150 --> 00:34:45.690
than a man.

00:34:45.690 --> 00:34:48.719
And most films don't
pass that simple test.

00:34:48.719 --> 00:34:51.449
So I thought I would start
one called the Gold Test.

00:34:51.449 --> 00:34:53.909
Is there a character
with a disability

00:34:53.909 --> 00:34:59.190
in this fictional story that
is three-dimensional and not

00:34:59.190 --> 00:35:01.680
defined by their disability?

00:35:01.680 --> 00:35:06.150
To my surprise,
there wasn't anybody.

00:35:06.150 --> 00:35:09.240
There wasn't anybody
in the film whatsoever

00:35:09.240 --> 00:35:12.360
in these fictitious
worlds that we're seeing.

00:35:12.360 --> 00:35:16.170
So my thought is, if you don't
exist in our worlds of fiction,

00:35:16.170 --> 00:35:18.372
how do you exist in real life?

00:35:18.372 --> 00:35:19.830
When you're going
out for that job,

00:35:19.830 --> 00:35:24.090
how does that employer know to
hire you if they haven't even

00:35:24.090 --> 00:35:26.640
seen you in works of fiction?

00:35:26.640 --> 00:35:29.940
If we look at stereotypes
that we hold--

00:35:29.940 --> 00:35:33.790
and we all hold
stereotypes about people.

00:35:33.790 --> 00:35:36.540
And if we look at
what the stereotype is

00:35:36.540 --> 00:35:41.550
of people with disabilities,
it's not really good.

00:35:41.550 --> 00:35:46.440
But it is really our
industry's responsibility

00:35:46.440 --> 00:35:50.100
to not only hire people
behind the scenes

00:35:50.100 --> 00:35:56.250
or in front of the camera,
but to produce media embracing

00:35:56.250 --> 00:36:04.350
that we are members of society
who are vibrant and add so much

00:36:04.350 --> 00:36:06.720
and are incredibly capable.

00:36:06.720 --> 00:36:09.120
You know, myself,
Jenni, and Jim,

00:36:09.120 --> 00:36:11.430
we spend so much
of our time talking

00:36:11.430 --> 00:36:15.360
about what makes us diverse
when if you think about it, what

00:36:15.360 --> 00:36:18.660
makes us diverse, we
had no say in being born

00:36:18.660 --> 00:36:20.310
with these disabilities, right?

00:36:20.310 --> 00:36:22.120
An area that we
did have a say in

00:36:22.120 --> 00:36:24.660
is the area that we
studied and we practice.

00:36:24.660 --> 00:36:26.520
But we're still
not being brought

00:36:26.520 --> 00:36:30.003
to the table for the area that
we spent many years studying.

00:36:30.003 --> 00:36:31.920
We're still being brought
to the table saying,

00:36:31.920 --> 00:36:34.950
oh, hey, let's talk about
what makes you diverse, right?

00:36:34.950 --> 00:36:38.040
And I think we need to have
these conversations to get over

00:36:38.040 --> 00:36:41.850
this hurdle to eventually
shift the focus on our work.

00:36:41.850 --> 00:36:43.690
But I think this is
the start of that.

00:36:43.690 --> 00:36:48.060
And I think with the climate of
all the other activism that's

00:36:48.060 --> 00:36:50.910
going on right now, we really
need to harness this energy

00:36:50.910 --> 00:36:53.430
and use it for good
and say, hey, it's

00:36:53.430 --> 00:36:55.770
not OK that we're not even 1%.

00:36:55.770 --> 00:36:58.770
You know, please help
us achieve parity.

00:36:58.770 --> 00:37:01.530
What do you think that
the studios need to do?

00:37:01.530 --> 00:37:04.530
The start of that is just
asking questions, right?

00:37:04.530 --> 00:37:06.060
Asking thoughtful questions.

00:37:06.060 --> 00:37:10.290
I had a producer once who hired
me to visual effects on set.

00:37:10.290 --> 00:37:13.060
And they said, hey,
so this is going

00:37:13.060 --> 00:37:16.350
to be my first time working with
someone in a wheelchair, right?

00:37:16.350 --> 00:37:17.700
This is a new realm for me.

00:37:17.700 --> 00:37:19.390
And I'm open to learning.

00:37:19.390 --> 00:37:22.080
Can you tell me what
I can do to help?

00:37:22.080 --> 00:37:23.670
Is there anything
you need, right?

00:37:23.670 --> 00:37:28.600
How can I be a best support for
you to do your job well on set?

00:37:28.600 --> 00:37:30.870
That's something I've
been wanting to say,

00:37:30.870 --> 00:37:32.880
and I really love what you said.

00:37:32.880 --> 00:37:35.010
If I'm going to hire somebody--

00:37:35.010 --> 00:37:36.870
I'm non-disabled.

00:37:36.870 --> 00:37:39.210
Instead of, like,
being on the negative,

00:37:39.210 --> 00:37:42.660
like, well, just tell me
what you won't be able to do.

00:37:42.660 --> 00:37:45.870
Tell me how I can--

00:37:45.870 --> 00:37:48.180
is there anything I
can do to help you

00:37:48.180 --> 00:37:50.580
do the best work that you can?

00:37:50.580 --> 00:37:52.590
I don't think I said
that quite eloquently.

00:37:52.590 --> 00:37:54.100
But you get the point.

00:37:54.100 --> 00:37:57.390
And it's just-- it's
a different attitude.

00:37:57.390 --> 00:38:02.610
None of us get to your door
with being oversensitive and mad

00:38:02.610 --> 00:38:03.510
at everybody.

00:38:03.510 --> 00:38:06.510
We've gotten to you
with being able to deal

00:38:06.510 --> 00:38:08.040
with our disability.

00:38:08.040 --> 00:38:10.630
And we are comfortable
with our disability.

00:38:10.630 --> 00:38:13.195
So it's like, ask the question.

00:38:13.195 --> 00:38:14.570
Let's just get it
taken care of--

00:38:14.570 --> 00:38:19.320
and I can be the biggest help
that you're going to have.

00:38:19.320 --> 00:38:21.420
Let's talk about
what advice you might

00:38:21.420 --> 00:38:26.190
give to young people coming
into the business today.

00:38:26.190 --> 00:38:29.610
I think there's kind of a
universal piece of advice

00:38:29.610 --> 00:38:31.460
for just about any question.

00:38:31.460 --> 00:38:34.220
And that is to find
your community.

00:38:34.220 --> 00:38:36.430
And that is to find your tribe.

00:38:36.430 --> 00:38:38.770
There are people like myself
and plenty of other people

00:38:38.770 --> 00:38:41.500
that have already
been doing this work.

00:38:41.500 --> 00:38:45.430
There is a group that I
helped found called FWD-Doc.

00:38:45.430 --> 00:38:49.630
And it is a group of documentary
filmmakers with disabilities.

00:38:49.630 --> 00:38:51.700
We have over 100 members now.

00:38:51.700 --> 00:38:55.780
And I would say that if you
can't live without it, then

00:38:55.780 --> 00:38:57.510
you gotta go and do it.

00:38:57.510 --> 00:38:59.950
You know, I always said to
myself when I was little,

00:38:59.950 --> 00:39:01.780
wouldn't it be
great if I can find

00:39:01.780 --> 00:39:05.830
a female Asian-American
woman who

00:39:05.830 --> 00:39:08.260
wants to go into visual
effects so I can figure out

00:39:08.260 --> 00:39:09.550
how she's done it, you know?

00:39:09.550 --> 00:39:12.970
And I hope that others
can take the lessons

00:39:12.970 --> 00:39:15.910
I've learned along the way
to say that it is possible.

00:39:15.910 --> 00:39:17.980
That's the beauty
of visual effects.

00:39:17.980 --> 00:39:20.470
Anything is possible.

00:39:20.470 --> 00:39:24.860
If they can make the
Millennium Falcon possible,

00:39:24.860 --> 00:39:28.390
making ramps to make
other studios accessible

00:39:28.390 --> 00:39:30.230
is also possible.

00:39:30.230 --> 00:39:31.120
Don't give up.

00:39:31.120 --> 00:39:33.250
It's wonderful that
kids today don't

00:39:33.250 --> 00:39:36.670
have to be the pioneers that
maybe the three of us were.

00:39:36.670 --> 00:39:37.990
The groundwork is laid.

00:39:37.990 --> 00:39:40.720
Hopefully we've laid
some more today.

00:39:40.720 --> 00:39:41.320
Thank you all.

00:39:41.320 --> 00:39:44.580
This has been a wonderful
opportunity to chat.

00:39:44.580 --> 00:39:48.030
So thank you guys
for joining us.

00:39:48.030 --> 00:39:51.030
In an era of celebrating
intersectionality,

00:39:51.030 --> 00:39:55.380
let's not exclude disability
from the conversation.

00:39:55.380 --> 00:39:58.140
Persons with disabilities
make up more than 20%

00:39:58.140 --> 00:40:01.510
of the world's population and
come from all backgrounds,

00:40:01.510 --> 00:40:05.670
including various
ethnicities, genders, races,

00:40:05.670 --> 00:40:08.430
and sexualities.

00:40:08.430 --> 00:40:11.970
What we see in motion pictures
influences the way we interact

00:40:11.970 --> 00:40:14.490
with the world around us?

00:40:14.490 --> 00:40:18.660
In paving the way for a new
generation of storytellers,

00:40:18.660 --> 00:40:22.500
let's create and promote stories
from all these intersecting

00:40:22.500 --> 00:40:26.520
backgrounds by hiring talent
with lived experiences

00:40:26.520 --> 00:40:29.240
to tell these stories.

00:40:29.240 --> 00:40:33.740
For our last panel, let's
hear from animator, director,

00:40:33.740 --> 00:40:39.650
writer Jorge Gutierrez and
actors Millicent Simmonds

00:40:39.650 --> 00:40:42.770
and Zack Gottsagen.
Who will speak

00:40:42.770 --> 00:40:49.720
about Hollywood's untapped
talent and unlimited potential.

00:40:49.720 --> 00:40:53.260
Fresh Voices: Hollywood's
Untapped Talent and Unlimited

00:40:53.260 --> 00:40:56.800
Potential with animation
director Jorge Gutierrez

00:40:56.800 --> 00:41:01.390
and actors Millicent
Simmonds and Zack Gottsagen.

00:41:01.390 --> 00:41:02.300
Hi, everyone.

00:41:02.300 --> 00:41:03.580
I am Jorge Gutierrez.

00:41:03.580 --> 00:41:07.840
I am the creator with my wife,
Sandra Equihua of a cartoon

00:41:07.840 --> 00:41:08.950
called El Tigre.

00:41:08.950 --> 00:41:11.740
I'm also the director and the
co-writer of a movie called

00:41:11.740 --> 00:41:13.000
The Book of Life.

00:41:13.000 --> 00:41:15.310
And currently I'm
writing and directing

00:41:15.310 --> 00:41:19.210
a Netflix movie series
called Maya and the Three.

00:41:19.210 --> 00:41:22.690
It's my pleasure to welcome
the very talented Millicent

00:41:22.690 --> 00:41:25.390
Simmonds, the star
of Wonderstruck

00:41:25.390 --> 00:41:27.460
and the blockbuster
A Quiet Place

00:41:27.460 --> 00:41:29.680
and the forthcoming
A Quiet Place Part

00:41:29.680 --> 00:41:33.130
II which looks so good.

00:41:33.130 --> 00:41:34.360
Hi.

00:41:34.360 --> 00:41:38.260
My name is Millicent
Simmonds, and I'm an actress.

00:41:38.260 --> 00:41:41.170
Currently I'm sitting in
a room with a fireplace

00:41:41.170 --> 00:41:43.130
and a TV mounted on the wall.

00:41:43.130 --> 00:41:45.010
It's a bright, cheery room.

00:41:45.010 --> 00:41:47.890
I'm wearing a black
long-sleeved turtleneck,

00:41:47.890 --> 00:41:50.360
and my hair is pulled
back in a low bun.

00:41:50.360 --> 00:41:51.700
I'm wearing a ring.

00:41:51.700 --> 00:41:55.750
I'm a Caucasian 17-year-old
female who is profoundly deaf.

00:41:55.750 --> 00:41:57.100
Thanks for joining us, Millie.

00:41:57.100 --> 00:42:00.190
And lastly, another extremely
talented young performer

00:42:00.190 --> 00:42:03.070
Zack Gottsagen is an
award-winning actor

00:42:03.070 --> 00:42:07.330
and the star of the 2019 indie
hit The Peanut Butter Falcon,

00:42:07.330 --> 00:42:08.770
which is amazing.

00:42:08.770 --> 00:42:19.720
My name is Zachary Gottsagen.
And I am the actor.

00:42:19.720 --> 00:42:31.740
And I'm fun and it's good to be
with my friends and my family.

00:42:31.740 --> 00:42:32.850
Cool.

00:42:32.850 --> 00:42:34.570
Thanks for joining us, Zack.

00:42:34.570 --> 00:42:37.080
I forgot to do my
visual description.

00:42:37.080 --> 00:42:43.290
I am a 45-year-old
lovably chubby Mexican man

00:42:43.290 --> 00:42:45.950
with a black mustache
and a hat that

00:42:45.950 --> 00:42:47.370
belonged to my grandfather.

00:42:47.370 --> 00:42:48.910
Let's kick things
off with the title

00:42:48.910 --> 00:42:53.160
of this discussion, Hollywood's
Untapped Talent and Unlimited

00:42:53.160 --> 00:42:54.180
Potential.

00:42:54.180 --> 00:42:58.350
What would you like to say to
our beloved industry and all

00:42:58.350 --> 00:43:03.150
these producers and studio
heads and directors about people

00:43:03.150 --> 00:43:05.580
like us that are a
little different,

00:43:05.580 --> 00:43:07.260
but we have so
much more to offer?

00:43:10.680 --> 00:43:14.010
The title of this
panel is so true.

00:43:14.010 --> 00:43:15.300
We do have talent.

00:43:15.300 --> 00:43:18.010
And we are proving it
time and time again.

00:43:18.010 --> 00:43:20.940
And we have been
throughout history.

00:43:20.940 --> 00:43:24.000
People with disabilities are
continuing to shock the world

00:43:24.000 --> 00:43:25.830
and to change
people's perspectives

00:43:25.830 --> 00:43:30.480
and open their
minds to diversity.

00:43:30.480 --> 00:43:34.530
We are breaking through all of
these boundaries and barriers.

00:43:34.530 --> 00:43:38.670
We are continuing to
educate people that --

00:43:38.670 --> 00:43:41.280
we're changing so much.

00:43:41.280 --> 00:43:45.972
There is no limit to
what we can do now.

00:43:45.972 --> 00:43:46.680
That's wonderful.

00:43:46.680 --> 00:43:48.810
Zack, do you want to chime in?

00:43:48.810 --> 00:44:03.380
I would say I'm good and excited
and just to show I'm talented.

00:44:03.380 --> 00:44:04.120
Absolutely.

00:44:04.120 --> 00:44:05.540
Yeah.

00:44:05.540 --> 00:44:07.510
I'm on the autism spectrum.

00:44:07.510 --> 00:44:09.710
You know, it's a thing
that early in my life

00:44:09.710 --> 00:44:11.540
brought me some difficulties.

00:44:11.540 --> 00:44:16.280
But it's actually
made me a lot more--

00:44:16.280 --> 00:44:17.990
I would say it's
become my superpower.

00:44:17.990 --> 00:44:20.910
And it's really allowed
me to be really,

00:44:20.910 --> 00:44:25.370
really sort of focused in my job
and really sort of inquisitive

00:44:25.370 --> 00:44:26.780
about the process.

00:44:26.780 --> 00:44:29.360
So that thing that
as a kid I was told

00:44:29.360 --> 00:44:33.980
was going to be a weakness has
turned out to be my strength.

00:44:33.980 --> 00:44:35.810
What do you look
for when reading

00:44:35.810 --> 00:44:37.730
a script for a new project?

00:44:37.730 --> 00:44:40.640
Have you had to fight
to play a role intended

00:44:40.640 --> 00:44:41.975
for a non-disabled person?

00:44:44.805 --> 00:44:46.430
When I'm reading
through a script, what

00:44:46.430 --> 00:44:51.590
I'm looking for is I have
to have that aha moment,

00:44:51.590 --> 00:44:55.280
that moment when I feel
goosebumps or I feel

00:44:55.280 --> 00:44:59.000
shocked because there is a twist
in the story or even a moment

00:44:59.000 --> 00:45:02.360
where I feel I resonate
with a particular character.

00:45:02.360 --> 00:45:03.410
I need to have that.

00:45:03.410 --> 00:45:06.290
I need to have that
connection with the character

00:45:06.290 --> 00:45:09.380
in order to immerse
myself in their world.

00:45:09.380 --> 00:45:13.880
And it helps me develop my
own character in my way.

00:45:13.880 --> 00:45:18.890
Now, I have had to compete
against non-disabled people

00:45:18.890 --> 00:45:20.840
for particular roles.

00:45:20.840 --> 00:45:23.150
I have had to audition.

00:45:23.150 --> 00:45:26.780
And again, when I'm
looking for a role,

00:45:26.780 --> 00:45:29.000
I try to find something
that might suit me.

00:45:29.000 --> 00:45:34.580
I'm a young teenage girl, 17,
and there might be a great role

00:45:34.580 --> 00:45:35.900
out there for me.

00:45:35.900 --> 00:45:38.870
I'm not above calling
directors and producers,

00:45:38.870 --> 00:45:43.100
suggesting they have a deaf
actress for a particular role.

00:45:43.100 --> 00:45:44.870
I don't know why we
can't make changes

00:45:44.870 --> 00:45:46.220
in scripts and in stories.

00:45:46.220 --> 00:45:48.380
It makes things
more interesting.

00:45:48.380 --> 00:45:50.930
On a side note, in the
movie A Quiet Place,

00:45:50.930 --> 00:45:55.880
John Krasinski intended
for the character Regan

00:45:55.880 --> 00:45:58.430
to be played by a deaf actress.

00:45:58.430 --> 00:46:01.820
And the producers didn't
want to have a deaf actress.

00:46:01.820 --> 00:46:05.120
They thought a hearing
actress would be acceptable.

00:46:05.120 --> 00:46:07.070
He fought for that
because he wanted

00:46:07.070 --> 00:46:09.560
to have an authentic
experience and to be

00:46:09.560 --> 00:46:11.930
able to have an
actress who understood

00:46:11.930 --> 00:46:15.320
what it felt like to be deaf
and how a deaf person would

00:46:15.320 --> 00:46:16.890
interact.

00:46:16.890 --> 00:46:19.670
And this is the reason why
it's important to have people

00:46:19.670 --> 00:46:21.530
with disabilities
playing characters

00:46:21.530 --> 00:46:23.780
who have disabilities
because it makes it

00:46:23.780 --> 00:46:26.110
feel more real and authentic.

00:46:26.110 --> 00:46:28.520
Well, I work in animation.

00:46:28.520 --> 00:46:31.340
And the medium in
animation lends itself

00:46:31.340 --> 00:46:34.880
to creating characters of all
types, especially characters

00:46:34.880 --> 00:46:35.810
with disabilities.

00:46:35.810 --> 00:46:40.910
And I really believe it's our
responsibility, especially

00:46:40.910 --> 00:46:44.390
someone like me, a director or
writer, to acknowledge people

00:46:44.390 --> 00:46:47.420
with disabilities and
put them in the stories

00:46:47.420 --> 00:46:49.760
and in these films, because
that's the world we live in.

00:46:49.760 --> 00:46:53.420
And I also believe that
through the power of animation,

00:46:53.420 --> 00:46:55.620
a lot of it is for children.

00:46:55.620 --> 00:47:00.380
So when you plant those
seeds and you basically

00:47:00.380 --> 00:47:03.350
illuminate children
to see disabled people

00:47:03.350 --> 00:47:04.770
as normal people--

00:47:04.770 --> 00:47:08.840
and one of the things that
I find a little intriguing

00:47:08.840 --> 00:47:12.050
is a lot of times the disabled
characters are perfect.

00:47:12.050 --> 00:47:15.140
But what I really like is
when the disabled characters

00:47:15.140 --> 00:47:18.050
have flaws because
that's humanity, right?

00:47:18.050 --> 00:47:22.040
The flaws are what reflects
a real depth of a person,

00:47:22.040 --> 00:47:26.000
because everyone who's disabled
isn't instantly a saint.

00:47:26.000 --> 00:47:28.320
We all have our
goods and our bads.

00:47:28.320 --> 00:47:31.190
So I think it's really,
really important to showcase

00:47:31.190 --> 00:47:34.370
that depth and that humanity.

00:47:34.370 --> 00:47:37.040
How do you feel your
success in Hollywood

00:47:37.040 --> 00:47:41.170
has been received by the public
and by the disabled community?

00:47:43.990 --> 00:47:46.960
I feel like when people
started taking notice

00:47:46.960 --> 00:47:51.730
of me and my career, most people
didn't know that I'm actually

00:47:51.730 --> 00:47:53.560
deaf in real life.

00:47:53.560 --> 00:47:57.190
And people are typically
very surprised by that.

00:47:57.190 --> 00:47:58.810
And they say things
like, oh, my gosh,

00:47:58.810 --> 00:48:00.460
I had no idea that you're deaf.

00:48:00.460 --> 00:48:02.680
You act really well.

00:48:02.680 --> 00:48:07.180
And you're good enough
to be in the movies.

00:48:07.180 --> 00:48:08.950
It's always shocking.

00:48:08.950 --> 00:48:11.140
And I'm never sure
how I feel when

00:48:11.140 --> 00:48:13.030
I receive responses like that.

00:48:13.030 --> 00:48:15.250
In some sense, I'm
excited that I've

00:48:15.250 --> 00:48:19.310
convinced them through my acting
skills to believe my character.

00:48:19.310 --> 00:48:22.510
But in another sense, I feel
a little bit disappointed

00:48:22.510 --> 00:48:25.600
and that I expect people
should know better.

00:48:25.600 --> 00:48:28.750
A deaf character should
be played by a deaf actor

00:48:28.750 --> 00:48:31.030
or a deaf actress.

00:48:31.030 --> 00:48:32.990
But it doesn't happen that way.

00:48:32.990 --> 00:48:34.970
A lot of people don't know that.

00:48:34.970 --> 00:48:38.650
And it's really tough for me.

00:48:38.650 --> 00:48:40.540
As far as the disability
community goes,

00:48:40.540 --> 00:48:45.060
I feel that the success I've
had has helped them as well.

00:48:45.060 --> 00:48:47.600
They're setting
their goals higher

00:48:47.600 --> 00:48:49.850
and achieving those goals.

00:48:49.850 --> 00:48:54.170
And from time to time, I
have fans reach out to me.

00:48:54.170 --> 00:48:57.890
And they thank me for
instilling confidence in them.

00:48:57.890 --> 00:49:00.470
They say when they see
themselves represented

00:49:00.470 --> 00:49:03.500
in Hollywood on
the silver screen,

00:49:03.500 --> 00:49:06.500
it's just saying that
we are smart enough.

00:49:06.500 --> 00:49:07.730
We are talented.

00:49:07.730 --> 00:49:09.770
We are good enough to do it.

00:49:09.770 --> 00:49:12.475
We can be successful.

00:49:12.475 --> 00:49:13.850
We can break
through the barriers

00:49:13.850 --> 00:49:15.800
that have been set for us.

00:49:15.800 --> 00:49:17.870
And that's not
just in Hollywood,

00:49:17.870 --> 00:49:20.750
but in so many
other areas as well,

00:49:20.750 --> 00:49:24.500
whether it's technology
or science or media.

00:49:24.500 --> 00:49:28.660
And it's so inspiring.

00:49:28.660 --> 00:49:33.300
Well, I am going to say about--

00:49:33.300 --> 00:49:39.570
those people do love me.

00:49:39.570 --> 00:49:44.860
And they do love my movie.

00:49:44.860 --> 00:49:51.790
And I got many awards.

00:49:51.790 --> 00:49:53.230
Awesome.

00:49:53.230 --> 00:49:56.380
You know, I'll tell
you guys, when I first

00:49:56.380 --> 00:49:58.780
was diagnosed with autism,
I was 40 years old.

00:49:58.780 --> 00:50:01.720
So I didn't know
until much later

00:50:01.720 --> 00:50:03.490
because my son has autism.

00:50:03.490 --> 00:50:04.990
And my parents said,
well, he's just

00:50:04.990 --> 00:50:06.820
like you when you were a kid.

00:50:06.820 --> 00:50:10.660
And then as soon as I found
out, the people who represent me

00:50:10.660 --> 00:50:14.110
said, you shouldn't
tell anybody.

00:50:14.110 --> 00:50:17.620
It might be something that makes
people think you're difficult.

00:50:17.620 --> 00:50:21.370
You know, all the cliches
and all the bad stereotypes.

00:50:21.370 --> 00:50:25.040
And so I decided, no, I think
my work speaks for itself.

00:50:25.040 --> 00:50:26.650
I think what I do
speaks for itself.

00:50:26.650 --> 00:50:29.380
I'm going to be super
open about my diagnosis.

00:50:29.380 --> 00:50:32.800
And I wanted to be a really
good example for my son

00:50:32.800 --> 00:50:34.390
when he grows up.

00:50:34.390 --> 00:50:38.830
And what that has turned
into is the autism community

00:50:38.830 --> 00:50:39.910
has embraced me.

00:50:39.910 --> 00:50:42.010
And I didn't even
know I was a part

00:50:42.010 --> 00:50:44.590
of such a loving community.

00:50:44.590 --> 00:50:48.920
I am now adding characters
with autism in my work.

00:50:48.920 --> 00:50:52.180
And I'm definitely--
in animation,

00:50:52.180 --> 00:50:55.515
I joke that 50% of the people
are on the autism spectrum,

00:50:55.515 --> 00:50:56.140
but they just--

00:50:56.140 --> 00:50:57.350
they've never been tested.

00:50:57.350 --> 00:50:59.530
But anyways, what
stories would you

00:50:59.530 --> 00:51:02.560
like to see told in
the future and by whom?

00:51:06.950 --> 00:51:10.460
I would love to see
stories about deaf icons

00:51:10.460 --> 00:51:14.810
and other icons in the
disability community.

00:51:14.810 --> 00:51:19.160
We already know who they
are and where they are now,

00:51:19.160 --> 00:51:22.640
but we don't know what
their backstory is.

00:51:22.640 --> 00:51:25.430
We don't know what
their life was like

00:51:25.430 --> 00:51:30.170
and what challenges and
obstacles they may have faced.

00:51:30.170 --> 00:51:34.780
I would love to see those
icons who have so much

00:51:34.780 --> 00:51:40.300
knowledge and experience and
the ability to tell a story

00:51:40.300 --> 00:51:43.000
and to let us connect with
them and to do the disability

00:51:43.000 --> 00:51:44.110
community justice.

00:51:44.110 --> 00:51:47.510
I would really like to see that.

00:51:47.510 --> 00:51:51.330
I would also love to see
stories from all over the world.

00:51:51.330 --> 00:51:54.590
I would love to hear
about people's culture

00:51:54.590 --> 00:51:58.580
and their language
and their traditions.

00:51:58.580 --> 00:52:01.490
It's an opportunity for us
to all educate ourselves

00:52:01.490 --> 00:52:03.660
and to learn from other people.

00:52:03.660 --> 00:52:16.270
I am going to say the romantic
story and of course comedy,

00:52:16.270 --> 00:52:27.670
drama, a superhero
with disabilities.

00:52:27.670 --> 00:52:31.410
Superhero with disabilities--
that would be incredible, Zack.

00:52:31.410 --> 00:52:32.170
Oh, my god.

00:52:32.170 --> 00:52:33.490
I want to make that movie.

00:52:33.490 --> 00:52:37.900
What does the future of
representation and inclusion

00:52:37.900 --> 00:52:41.230
in Hollywood hopefully
look like to you?

00:52:41.230 --> 00:52:46.420
I'm going to say
about the inclusion--

00:52:46.420 --> 00:52:54.700
this is about everybody
with disabilities.

00:52:54.700 --> 00:53:02.380
They really want to have
a really good chance.

00:53:02.380 --> 00:53:06.370
I would love to see a lot more
opportunity for people like us.

00:53:06.370 --> 00:53:08.830
I'd like to see more
movies that focus

00:53:08.830 --> 00:53:14.110
on not only having a character
with a disability viewed

00:53:14.110 --> 00:53:19.540
as sympathetic or less than
and more emphasis on characters

00:53:19.540 --> 00:53:23.170
with disabilities
having more hope

00:53:23.170 --> 00:53:25.660
and for the people
watching these movies

00:53:25.660 --> 00:53:29.060
and shows to become educated
and to learn and to actually

00:53:29.060 --> 00:53:29.560
listen.

00:53:32.140 --> 00:53:36.670
I would love to see more
barriers broken down.

00:53:36.670 --> 00:53:38.800
And I want to see
more opportunities

00:53:38.800 --> 00:53:43.740
for people in the community
to reach their dreams.

00:53:43.740 --> 00:53:45.010
I want to see diversity.

00:53:45.010 --> 00:53:49.830
I want to see varying languages
and backgrounds and everything

00:53:49.830 --> 00:53:50.880
on screen.

00:53:50.880 --> 00:53:53.550
I want every person
to feel like they're

00:53:53.550 --> 00:53:56.880
represented on the big
screen and for people

00:53:56.880 --> 00:53:59.310
to feel that they could
even write their own stories

00:53:59.310 --> 00:54:02.390
and share them with the world.

00:54:02.390 --> 00:54:03.680
Great.

00:54:03.680 --> 00:54:07.220
My dream in
representation would be so

00:54:07.220 --> 00:54:10.910
that disability
characters are normal,

00:54:10.910 --> 00:54:13.040
and they're in genre movies.

00:54:13.040 --> 00:54:15.560
There's a Western, and one of
the characters is disabled.

00:54:15.560 --> 00:54:16.977
And no one makes
a big deal of it.

00:54:16.977 --> 00:54:17.960
It's just normal.

00:54:17.960 --> 00:54:21.620
Or a sci-fi movie with someone,
one of the Fast and the Furious

00:54:21.620 --> 00:54:23.330
movies, and someone's
in a wheelchair,

00:54:23.330 --> 00:54:25.950
and it's normal, because
that's the real world.

00:54:25.950 --> 00:54:27.920
So that's what I
would love to see.

00:54:27.920 --> 00:54:31.460
What advice would you
like to give young people

00:54:31.460 --> 00:54:35.330
with disabilities who want to
break into our beloved film

00:54:35.330 --> 00:54:38.010
industry?

00:54:38.010 --> 00:54:41.370
My advice for anyone who wants
to pursue acting as a career

00:54:41.370 --> 00:54:44.250
is to have confidence
in yourself.

00:54:44.250 --> 00:54:47.340
Get yourself out of
your comfort zone

00:54:47.340 --> 00:54:50.670
and truly believe
that you can make it,

00:54:50.670 --> 00:54:53.350
because no one's going
to do that for you.

00:54:53.350 --> 00:54:55.957
You're the only one who
can do that for yourself.

00:54:55.957 --> 00:54:58.290
I would encourage you to get
involved in acting classes,

00:54:58.290 --> 00:55:00.780
whether it be in school
or in the community.

00:55:00.780 --> 00:55:04.170
Maybe you could try Broadway
and see how different

00:55:04.170 --> 00:55:07.380
that experience is with
the facial expressions

00:55:07.380 --> 00:55:08.730
and the acting approaches.

00:55:08.730 --> 00:55:10.290
Read as many books as you can.

00:55:10.290 --> 00:55:12.870
Watch as many videos as
you can get your hands on.

00:55:12.870 --> 00:55:14.492
Watch directors' cuts.

00:55:14.492 --> 00:55:16.200
Watch different movies
that are out there

00:55:16.200 --> 00:55:19.520
and see what it takes.

00:55:19.520 --> 00:55:21.890
You really have to do
the work for yourself.

00:55:21.890 --> 00:55:23.660
You could write
your own story even,

00:55:23.660 --> 00:55:27.860
because writing your own story
allows you to say what you want

00:55:27.860 --> 00:55:30.710
and to share your own
vision in your own career.

00:55:30.710 --> 00:55:34.670
And you can decide what people
might want to know about you.

00:55:34.670 --> 00:55:37.962
And that's what I think
is truly important.

00:55:37.962 --> 00:55:39.850
Wow.

00:55:39.850 --> 00:55:43.820
The advice I love to give
is no one's born a director.

00:55:43.820 --> 00:55:47.570
You become a director, and
you grow into these roles

00:55:47.570 --> 00:55:48.670
through hard work.

00:55:48.670 --> 00:55:52.970
But it's-- I always say if I
can do it, anybody can do it.

00:55:52.970 --> 00:55:57.980
I'm going to say
follow your heart.

00:55:57.980 --> 00:55:59.960
Follow your dreams.

00:55:59.960 --> 00:56:05.496
And someday, they will go big.

00:56:05.496 --> 00:56:07.450
Wow.

00:56:07.450 --> 00:56:08.770
That's beautiful.

00:56:08.770 --> 00:56:11.950
So with talent like you
two, I think the future

00:56:11.950 --> 00:56:13.690
is looking so bright.

00:56:13.690 --> 00:56:16.150
I want to thank all
our guests today.

